INT. TRANSIT AUTHORITY LOST-PROPERTY OFFICE - DAY

Shelves of umbrellas, gloves, a prosthetic leg. Fluorescent tubes. A counter with a bell.

OPAL REINHARDT (40s, cardigan, lanyard) sorts a bin of scarves, folding each into a neat square, one at a time, with the slow rhythm of someone who has not slept. At the counter, an OLDER MAN (70s, flat cap, grocery bag) waits, rocking on his heels, patient, polite, watching her.

OPAL
Be right with you.

OLDER MAN
No rush.

She finishes the scarf. Moves to the next. He glances at the shelves, at the umbrellas, at the leg, and back at her.

OPAL (CONT'D)
What did you lose?

OLDER MAN
A coat.

OPAL
Color?

OLDER MAN
Gray. Wool. My wife's initials in the lining.

OPAL
Date?

OLDER MAN
Fourteenth. On the Number 9.

She crosses to a rack, runs her fingers along the hangers, and stops at a gray coat. She lifts it, turns it inside out, finds the stitched letters. She says nothing for several seconds.

OPAL (CONT'D)
What initials?

OLDER MAN
M.R.

She lowers the coat onto the counter, smoothing the sleeves. Her hands tremble.

OPAL
Sir. What's your name?

OLDER MAN
Ambrose Reinhardt.

She closes her eyes. Opens them. Looks at him properly for the first time. He looks back at her, and something in his posture shifts, softens, as though he has recognized her also.

AMBROSE
Opal?

OPAL
Hello, Dad.

He takes the coat. He doesn't put it on. He holds it against his chest while the bell above the door jingles for someone else entirely.
